<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Le Corbusier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Charles-Édouard Jeanneret</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Le Corbusier was a Swiss-born French architect, designer and urban planner and one of the pioneers of modern architecture. His buildings, such as the Villa Savoye, the Unité d'Habitation in Marseille and the chapel at Ronchamp, and his plans for Chandigarh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> shaped 20th-century design. He admired the plain, functional forms of the Mzab towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, which he visited in the 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/le-corbusier.jpg" alt="Le Corbusier" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joop van Bilsen for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Corbusier_(1964).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
